#pass and continue statements in while loop
#pass statement is used when you want to write a placeholder for a block of code that will be implemented later. It does nothing and is used to avoid syntax errors when a statement is required but you do not want to execute any code.
#continue statement is used to skip the current iteration of a loop and move to the next iteration. It is used when you want to skip certain conditions or when you want to avoid executing certain code for specific iterations.
#break statement is used to exit a loop prematurely when a certain condition is met. It is used to stop the execution of a loop when a specific condition is satisfied, allowing the program to continue with the next part of the code after the loop.
for i in range(10):
    if i == 5:
        continue
    print(i)
    
for i in range(10):
    if i == 5:
        pass # pass is a placeholder statement that does nothing. It is used when a statement is required syntactically but you do not want any code to be executed.
    print(i)
    
    

